========================================================================
PART 3b: Off-grid vs on-grid. The multiple-of-14 runner sits in
   section 0 at EVERY a/14 (||14m * a/14|| = ||m*a|| = 0).
   So on the GRID a/14 the covering core is never lonely; M is
   attained OFF-grid. Verify the optimum tau is NOT of form a/14.
========================================================================
  {1..11,13,84}  (84=6*14)       tau=   37/89  denom=89   on-grid(=a/14)? False
  {1..13,14}     (14=1*14)       tau=    1/15  denom=15   on-grid(=a/14)? False
  {1..13,28}     (28=2*14)       tau=    2/29  denom=29   on-grid(=a/14)? False
  {1..11,13,14}                  tau=    1/12  denom=12   on-grid(=a/14)? False
  {1..13,42}     (42=3*14)       tau=    3/43  denom=43   on-grid(=a/14)? False
  {1..9,11,13,84}                tau=    1/10  denom=10   on-grid(=a/14)? False

========================================================================
PART 4: Stress test the CORE CLAIM across MANY random configs:
   is M ALWAYS attained at a tau with >=2 binding runners that form
   a sum/diff pair? Report any counterexample.
========================================================================

Tested 4000 random configs (n=2..8).
Configs where NO optimum is a binding-pair-crossing: 0
  ...of those, where every optimum has a SINGLE binding runner: 0

Examples where the optimum is pinned by a SINGLE runner (half-integer),
NOT a pair (these are honest exceptions to a naive 'always a pair' claim):

========================================================================
PART 4b: Refined claim test. A SINGLE binding runner v at tau=(2k+1)/(2v)
  means ||v tau||=1/2, the MAX possible. That only pins M when 1/2 is
  the min, i.e. ALL runners are at 1/2 => M=1/2 (only S={powers giving
  half}). Otherwise the min is set by a runner NOT at 1/2. Test: when
  M<1/2, is the binding set ALWAYS >=2 and contain a sum/diff pair?
========================================================================

Tested 5576 configs with 0<M<1/2.
Configs where NO optimum has a sum/diff binding pair: 0
==> NO counterexamples: when 0<M<1/2, the optimum ALWAYS has a
    sum/diff binding pair (REFINED CLAIM holds on this sample).
